  "answer": "To reduce the risk of reinforcement-learning agents breaking safety constraints (causing accidents) while exploring online when their transition dynamics change from factors like joint failures or battery depletion, it is proposed to deploy a recovery RL agent that overrides unsafe actions using a safety critic threshold (https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03575). The mechanism is based on the theoretical insight that while transition dynamics change, the specific states that violate constraints usually do not, meaning safety knowledge can be reused across environments (https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03575). First, a safety critic is meta-learned offline across diverse datasets using Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning (MAML) to produce parameters optimized for adaptation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03575). This adapted critic is then used to gate actions during online Soft Actor-Critic (SAC) learning of the task while the critic and policies are continually updated (https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03575). Finally, at deployment, the task policy is executed normally except when the risk score exceeds a threshold (Q_risk > ε_risk), at which point actions from the recovery policy are taken instead (https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03575)."
